#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Choose and run the tests a change touches, before the gate, so a gate is not refused on them
(the operator's overnight item 9, 2026-11-12).

    scripts/nmas-pregate [--range A..B] [--message FILE] [--list] [--no-wait] [--repo DIR]
    scripts/nmas-pregate --verdict TREE-OR-COMMIT

The change is what is staged (`git diff --cached`), or the commits of `--range A..B`.

0. **A snapshot of the tested tree** (C3421; the operator, 2026-11-13: "make it mechanical
   rather than a promise: the pre-gate runs on a snapshot of the staged tree"). The tree is the
   index's (`git write-tree`), or B's with `--range`; it is checked out, as scripts/nmas-gate's
   step 2b does, into a worktree of its own in an owner-only temporary folder outside the
   checkout, its HEAD the change's base (HEAD, or A), so the selection, every run and the
   commit-msg hook read exactly that tree, and the working tree stays free to edit while it
   runs. scripts/nmas-gate's SNAPSHOT_LOCAL (parsed from it, one list) is copied in, less every
   file under `data/` (the operator's local-only files are never copied): the tests that read
   those (CHECKOUT_ONLY) are deselected in the snapshot and run in the checkout, reading the
   working tree's files, and the summary says so. The snapshot is removed whatever happens; one
   a killed run left is removed by the next run once its owner is gone.
1. **The tests, each with why.** A reason is parsed, never a substring of prose:
   - a test file that imports a changed Python module (its `import` and `from ... import`
     statements, read by `ast`, anywhere in the file), or names it as a whole string literal;
   - a test file naming a changed file by a whole string literal: its path, a path ending in
     `/<its path>`, a template's name under `templates/`, or, for a template, a script or a
     static file, its file name (`"_attention.html"`, `"nmas-gate"`);
   - a test file naming, by a string literal anchored to the whole rule, a route that renders a
     changed template (or a template that includes, extends or imports it), or a route of
     `routes/` whose function the change touches; and a BROWSER file naming a route whose
     page loads such a route by htmx (`hx-get`, `hx-post`, `src`, `href`, `action`);
   - a changed shared filter, global, test or context name (C3421): a function registered with
     Jinja (`app_template_filter`, `template_global`, `add_template_filter`, an
     `app_context_processor`'s returned names, an assignment to `jinja_env.globals[...]` or
     `.filters[...]`; in routes/, app.py or any changed Python file) whose body, registration,
     or a name it reads the change touches, on either side: every template using it (parsed
     from its `{{ }}` and `{% %}` code) counts as changed for the route rule above, and with a
     changed template (a macro file, an included or imported partial) every template using it,
     transitively, chooses the test files naming it by a whole literal;
   - tests/test_requirements_lock.py (LOCK_TEST) when a changed Python file's imports of
     modules outside the product (neither the standard library nor a folder or module of the
     tree) gain or lose a name;
   - the changed test files themselves;
   - the fixed convention set (CONVENTIONS), the manual and fidelity tests (MANUAL) when
     `docs/manual/` or a template changes, and the route tests (ROUTES) when `routes/` does.
2. **The runs**, only while no gate runs: the full-suite lock names a live process (the same
   check the build briefs make), and then it waits, saying why every WAIT_EVERY_S, for at most
   WAIT_BOUND_S (`scripts/nmas-workers`' gate wait), or refuses at once with `--no-wait`.
   The confined files run through `scripts/nmas-test -n 2`; the BROWSER files (a file that
   imports `Browser` from `tests.browser`, read by `ast`) run unconfined, in CI's interpreter
   with the browser folder first on PATH and NMAS_REQUIRE_BROWSER=1, so a browser that cannot
   start fails, never skips. Each run is bounded (CONFINED_BOUND, BROWSER_BOUND).
3. **The message** (`--message FILE`): `scripts/hooks/commit-msg` on a copy of it, traced, so
   the check that refused is named.
4. **The summary**: each test file, why it was chosen, and passed, failed or NOT RUN (with
   why); a file none of whose tests ran is not run, and so is a whole run that did not happen.
   A file whose every test skipped is "skipped as declared" only when the skip check accepted
   every skip of the run as declared for the gate (tests/skip_reasons.py; the board shots, run
   only when asked): that is the state the gate holds it in, and it counts toward a pass. A
   skip check that refused or could not run leaves it not run (C3448).
5. **The verdict, per tree** (C3421): on every exit after the runs, a record keyed by the
   tested tree's id, written atomically (a temporary file in the same folder, then
   `os.replace`) to `<git common dir>/nmas-pregate/verdicts/<tree>.json`: the verdict (`pass`
   only when the exit is 0: every chosen file ran and passed, and the message's checks passed
   when `--message` was given; else `failed` or `not run`), the change (staged or the range)
   and its base, the time, each chosen file with why and its state, and the notes. A later run
   of the same tree replaces it. `--verdict TREE-OR-COMMIT` prints the record of that tree (a
   commit's tree) and exits 0 only for a pass: the proposed pre-push hook's question.

It writes nothing in the working tree: every run happens in the snapshot (the CHECKOUT_ONLY
tests excepted, said), and every run it starts has PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 and
`-p no:cacheprovider`, with pytest's folders and the JUnit files in a temporary folder outside
it. The working tree is fingerprinted before and after (`scripts/nmas-control`'s fingerprint)
and whether it changed is said; it decides nothing, since the tree tested is the snapshot's.
Edits not staged are said, since they are not in the tested tree.

Exit: 0 when every chosen file ran and passed (and the message's checks passed); 1 when a test
failed, a skip was not declared, or a message check refused; 2 when it cannot start (not a git
checkout, an unreadable range, no message file, no snapshot); 4 when anything chosen did not
run (a gate held the lock past the bound, a bound fired, no browser interpreter). `--list` runs
nothing, records no verdict, says so, and exits 0. `--verdict`: 0 a pass, 1 none or not a pass,
2 no such tree.

Cost: the chosen files' time, about a minute for the convention set (measured below).
Prevents: a 20-minute gate refused on a check a chosen run meets first (2026-11-12: a CLAUDE.md
quote, a device name in a docstring, tests of a touched template never run, a shared store's
leak, a missing Bypass trailer).
"""
